1
00:00:04,705 --> 00:00:07,874
And one can of cocoa
extract, if you would.

2
00:00:07,916 --> 00:00:09,543
[CLERK 19A]
Very good, sir.

3
00:00:09,585 --> 00:00:10,669
[MAN 19A]
Playing "Lord of Crime?"

4
00:00:10,711 --> 00:00:11,629
[LOUIS grunts]

5
00:00:11,670 --> 00:00:14,005
[MAN 19B]
Yes. My boy got carried away

6
00:00:14,047 --> 00:00:16,800
and apparently injured
one of the other lads.

7
00:00:16,841 --> 00:00:20,721
Seems imitating Robin Hood
is very popular these days.

8
00:00:20,763 --> 00:00:22,055
But this Lord of Crime,

9
00:00:22,097 --> 00:00:24,516
he's nothing but a
bloodthirsty hoodlum.

10
00:00:24,558 --> 00:00:26,435
[MAN 19A]
That he is.

11
00:00:26,477 --> 00:00:29,062
I wish the children would
start emulating this young MP,

12
00:00:29,104 --> 00:00:31,315
instead of those
bloody mobsters.

13
00:00:31,356 --> 00:00:33,150
[LOUIS]
MP?

14
00:02:11,289 --> 00:02:12,457
[PARLIAMENT MEMBER 19A]
Young upstart!

15
00:02:12,499 --> 00:02:13,333
[PARLIAMENT MEMBER 19B]
Who do you think you are?

16
00:02:13,375 --> 00:02:14,877
[SPEAKER 19A]
We will have order!

17
00:02:14,918 --> 00:02:16,920
Gentlemen, quiet, please!

18
00:02:16,961 --> 00:02:19,464
Mister Whiteley
still has the floor.

19
00:02:19,506 --> 00:02:21,717
You may continue.

20
00:02:23,468 --> 00:02:24,969
Oi, he's coming out!

21
00:02:25,011 --> 00:02:26,346
[REPORTERS shouting]

22
00:02:26,388 --> 00:02:27,514
[REPORTER 19B]
Mister Whiteley!

23
00:02:27,556 --> 00:02:29,057
Doesn't this make the third time

24
00:02:29,099 --> 00:02:31,685
you've proposed
your election reform bill?

25
00:02:31,727 --> 00:02:34,104
Assuming it even makes it
through the House of Commons,

26
00:02:34,145 --> 00:02:36,022
it's all but certain to be
killed when it reaches

27
00:02:36,064 --> 00:02:38,483
the House of Lords!
Isn't that right?

28
00:02:39,568 --> 00:02:41,361
[WHITELEY]
Before proposing the bill,

29
00:02:41,403 --> 00:02:44,114
I knew there would be
this level of opposition.

30
00:02:44,155 --> 00:02:46,658
However, for the
sake of this nation,

31
00:02:46,700 --> 00:02:49,453
the right to vote must
be extended to everyone,

32
00:02:49,494 --> 00:02:52,205
including working
class citizens.

33
00:02:52,247 --> 00:02:53,707
You have my word--

34
00:02:53,749 --> 00:02:57,711
whatever it takes, I'll see that
the measure passes this season.

35
00:02:57,753 --> 00:02:59,170
[REPORTER 19A] How will
you deal with the opposition

36
00:02:59,212 --> 00:03:00,631
within the House of Commons?

37
00:03:00,672 --> 00:03:02,716
[REPORTER 19C] And how will you
wear down the House of Lords?

38
00:03:02,758 --> 00:03:04,635
Do you have some
secret strategy?

39
00:03:04,676 --> 00:03:08,305
[WHITELEY] I will convince
them somehow. Quote me on that.

40
00:03:08,346 --> 00:03:10,014
Please, sir. Step inside.

41
00:03:10,056 --> 00:03:11,975
[WHITELEY]
Of course, thank you.

42
00:03:13,310 --> 00:03:14,311
[grunts]

43
00:03:14,352 --> 00:03:17,147
That smell... Gunpowder?

44
00:03:17,188 --> 00:03:18,774
Surely not.

45
00:03:19,733 --> 00:03:23,695
It's a bomb! Get away
from the carriage! Quickly!

46
00:03:25,322 --> 00:03:32,996
[CITIZENS screaming]

47
00:03:33,037 --> 00:03:35,874
[WHITELEY grunting]

48
00:03:35,916 --> 00:03:40,086
[CITIZENS murmuring]

49
00:03:42,255 --> 00:03:44,257
[gasps]

50
00:03:44,299 --> 00:03:47,177
[MAN 19C grunts]

51
00:03:47,218 --> 00:03:50,180
Tell me, who in
the blazes hired you?

52
00:03:50,221 --> 00:03:52,474
Whatever do you mean, sir?

53
00:03:52,516 --> 00:03:54,434
[POLICEMAN 19A] Mister
Whiteley! Are you all right?

54
00:03:54,476 --> 00:03:55,310
[WHITELEY]
Yes.

55
00:03:55,352 --> 00:03:57,312
But never mind me,
search this man.

56
00:03:57,354 --> 00:03:58,689
[POLICEMAN 19B]
I don't understand.

57
00:03:58,730 --> 00:04:00,106
Why this man, sir?

58
00:04:00,148 --> 00:04:01,817
On numerous occasions,

59
00:04:01,859 --> 00:04:05,236
I've had the chance to
address large crowds of people.

60
00:04:05,278 --> 00:04:07,113
However, when I look out,

61
00:04:07,155 --> 00:04:10,283
I can instantly tell by their
faces what they're thinking--

62
00:04:10,325 --> 00:04:12,243
their inner feelings.

63
00:04:12,285 --> 00:04:14,454
Faces brimming
with expectations,

64
00:04:14,496 --> 00:04:16,039
pinning their hopes on me.

65
00:04:16,080 --> 00:04:20,084
Faces of those who are
doubtful, sceptical of me.

66
00:04:20,126 --> 00:04:24,798
And faces like his,
that wish only death upon me.

67
00:04:24,840 --> 00:04:27,467
Look! I found some spare
fuses inside his bag!

68
00:04:27,509 --> 00:04:30,387
[CITIZENS gasp]

69
00:04:30,428 --> 00:04:32,472
Nicely done, Mister Whiteley.

70
00:04:32,514 --> 00:04:34,432
My dear constituents!

71
00:04:34,474 --> 00:04:36,560
Even when faced with
this sort of violence,

72
00:04:36,601 --> 00:04:38,520
I will never back down!

73
00:04:38,562 --> 00:04:41,940
I will get the reform bill
passed and that's a promise!

74
00:04:41,982 --> 00:04:43,358
Mark my words!

75
00:04:43,400 --> 00:04:45,569
Achieving equality
is not only my mission--

76
00:04:45,610 --> 00:04:48,572
it's the dream shared by the
people of this great nation!

77
00:04:48,613 --> 00:04:50,240
[CITIZENS gasp, cheer]

78
00:04:50,281 --> 00:04:51,449
[CITIZEN 19A]
Whiteley!

79
00:04:51,491 --> 00:04:52,659
[CITIZEN 19B]
Adam Whiteley!

80
00:04:52,701 --> 00:04:55,871
[CITIZEN 19C] You are
the delegate of the people!

81
00:05:01,167 --> 00:05:03,837
[LOUIS] "Assassination
attempt on MP Whiteley."

82
00:05:03,879 --> 00:05:06,924
Some say he staged it
himself to gain publicity.

83
00:05:06,965 --> 00:05:08,884
"Is Britain's white
knight nothing more

84
00:05:08,926 --> 00:05:11,469
than a swindler
peddling 'equality'?"

85
00:05:11,511 --> 00:05:13,388
As far as I'm able to tell,

86
00:05:13,430 --> 00:05:16,725
there's nothing dubious about
Mister Whiteley or his message.

87
00:05:16,767 --> 00:05:20,020
No, he's a man
of impeccable integrity.

88
00:05:20,062 --> 00:05:22,606
The question is,
how far does he intend to stride

89
00:05:22,647 --> 00:05:25,859
under this banner
of social equality?

90
00:05:25,901 --> 00:05:28,695
William, what do you think?

91
00:05:28,737 --> 00:05:30,488
The bomber was a mobster

92
00:05:30,530 --> 00:05:33,491
and most likely hired
by the House of Lords.

93
00:05:33,533 --> 00:05:35,326
Should the Yard
make the connection,

94
00:05:35,368 --> 00:05:36,954
Whiteley will become
the standard bearer

95
00:05:36,995 --> 00:05:39,122
for a popular revolution.

96
00:05:39,163 --> 00:05:41,291
Election reform will be swift,

97
00:05:41,332 --> 00:05:43,919
and the prime minister's
post will be his.

98
00:05:45,462 --> 00:05:49,091
[WILLIAM] On the other hand,
political and social unrest

99
00:05:49,132 --> 00:05:51,969
within the country
will only increase.

100
00:05:52,010 --> 00:05:54,429
And the lives of many
people could easily be lost

101
00:05:54,471 --> 00:05:56,723
in the ensuing
violence and mayhem.

102
00:05:57,849 --> 00:05:59,350
Whether or not
that is the outcome

103
00:05:59,392 --> 00:06:01,770
he is secretly hoping for

104
00:06:01,812 --> 00:06:04,272
is a matter I would
like to test him on.

105
00:06:04,314 --> 00:06:07,233
"Equality." Yeah, right.
Heard a lot of politicians

106
00:06:07,275 --> 00:06:09,193
talking about
those lofty ideals;

107
00:06:09,235 --> 00:06:10,988
none of them were on the level.

108
00:06:11,029 --> 00:06:12,280
[WILLIAM]
No.

109
00:06:12,322 --> 00:06:14,950
We cannot say with any
certainty he's like the others.

110
00:06:14,992 --> 00:06:15,993
[MORAN]
Hm?

111
00:06:16,034 --> 00:06:18,078
Depending on how
Whiteley reacts,

112
00:06:18,120 --> 00:06:20,455
this land might see
equality at last

113
00:06:20,497 --> 00:06:23,708
without the need for us to
become involved any further.

114
00:06:36,972 --> 00:06:38,682
Oi, it's time for your supper--

115
00:06:38,723 --> 00:06:39,683
[gasps]

116
00:06:39,724 --> 00:06:40,767
[plate clatters]

117
00:06:40,809 --> 00:06:42,310
[POLICEMAN 19C]
Wha--? Blimey!

118
00:06:42,352 --> 00:06:44,604
Somebody call a doctor!

119
00:06:45,897 --> 00:06:48,066
The bomber died while
in police custody?

120
00:06:48,108 --> 00:06:50,068
How is that even possible?

121
00:06:50,110 --> 00:06:52,362
[PATERSON grunts] He was
stabbed through the throat.

122
00:06:52,403 --> 00:06:54,614
The guard discovered
a pen within the cell.

123
00:06:54,656 --> 00:06:56,825
He'd already died
from blood loss.

124
00:06:56,867 --> 00:06:58,493
Based on the nature of death,

125
00:06:58,535 --> 00:07:01,913
the CID hasn't ruled it
as suicide or murder yet.

126
00:07:01,955 --> 00:07:04,791
What is going on here?

127
00:07:04,833 --> 00:07:06,626
I'm sorry such a terrible mishap

128
00:07:06,668 --> 00:07:11,089
occurred in my own station,
Mister Whiteley. Truly.

129
00:07:11,131 --> 00:07:13,424
[WHITELEY] It's hard to believe
a mobster working for money

130
00:07:13,466 --> 00:07:15,301
would take his own
life out of loyalty

131
00:07:15,343 --> 00:07:17,303
for the House of Lords.

132
00:07:17,345 --> 00:07:20,515
If it was homicide,
then the only conclusion

133
00:07:20,557 --> 00:07:24,728
is that there is a
collaborator within the Yard.

134
00:07:24,769 --> 00:07:26,521
[WHITELEY]
Chief Inspector Paterson,

135
00:07:26,563 --> 00:07:28,439
it is going to be
much more difficult now

136
00:07:28,481 --> 00:07:30,984
to determine who
hired our bomber.

137
00:07:31,026 --> 00:07:33,904
It gives me pause
to request this,

138
00:07:33,945 --> 00:07:36,198
but in regards to
the investigation,

139
00:07:36,239 --> 00:07:37,991
I would like to
select which personnel

140
00:07:38,033 --> 00:07:39,951
are involved with this case.

141
00:07:39,993 --> 00:07:42,120
Hand-picked by you?

142
00:07:42,162 --> 00:07:43,454
[WHITELEY]
Yes.

143
00:07:43,496 --> 00:07:47,959
But I assure you, I am a
rather good judge of character.

144
00:07:56,551 --> 00:07:58,178
[EMPLOYEE 19A]
This article simply won't do.

145
00:07:58,220 --> 00:07:59,470
We are a high-class--

146
00:07:59,512 --> 00:08:01,347
Oh, good morning,
Mister President, sir.

147
00:08:01,389 --> 00:08:05,309
[EMPLOYEES murmuring]

148
00:08:08,813 --> 00:08:11,858
A very pleasant morning
to you, Lord Milverton.

149
00:08:11,900 --> 00:08:14,778
Was your meeting at the
House of Lords productive?

150
00:08:14,819 --> 00:08:15,904
[MILVERTON]
Yes.

151
00:08:15,946 --> 00:08:18,823
A shame they didn't
come to me sooner, though.

152
00:08:18,865 --> 00:08:21,118
And not after sending
low-level thugs

153
00:08:21,159 --> 00:08:24,287
to carry out a failed
assassination plot.

154
00:08:24,328 --> 00:08:26,831
If they had succeeded
in killing Whiteley,

155
00:08:26,873 --> 00:08:28,332
he would have died a hero--

156
00:08:28,374 --> 00:08:31,335
a martyr for the great
cause of equality.

157
00:08:31,377 --> 00:08:33,880
What an utter pack of fools.

158
00:08:33,922 --> 00:08:36,382
Still, there may
be hope for them.

159
00:08:36,424 --> 00:08:39,094
The House of Lords
did see fit to hire me,

160
00:08:39,136 --> 00:08:41,679
so I'll dispose of him.

161
00:08:41,721 --> 00:08:46,059
And my approach will
be, shall we say, different.

162
00:08:46,101 --> 00:08:47,894
Then, I would assume...

163
00:08:47,936 --> 00:08:49,896
[MILVERTON]
Yes.

164
00:08:49,938 --> 00:08:52,774
I will destroy
Whiteley's good name,

165
00:08:52,816 --> 00:08:56,778
casting him down from
his righteous spot.

166
00:08:56,820 --> 00:08:59,614
Right then, when
the white knight falls,

167
00:08:59,656 --> 00:09:02,784
I'll be watching from
the best seat in the house.

168
00:09:10,541 --> 00:09:12,376
[WILLIAM]
It's time, Albert.

169
00:09:12,418 --> 00:09:15,255
I trust you've
prepared everything?

170
00:09:15,297 --> 00:09:19,092
Tomorrow we'll see what
Mister Whiteley is made of.

171
00:09:19,134 --> 00:09:20,593
[ALBERT]
Mm.

172
00:09:22,428 --> 00:09:26,141
[ALBERT] On that note, I have
the documents you requested.

173
00:09:26,183 --> 00:09:29,769
[WILLIAM] Excellent work,
brother. Thank you.

174
00:09:29,811 --> 00:09:31,688
[ALBERT grunts]

175
00:09:33,481 --> 00:09:35,566
I know this is
an essential step.

176
00:09:35,608 --> 00:09:36,734
I only hope, like us,

177
00:09:36,776 --> 00:09:40,738
he can move forward
despite the pain inflicted.

178
00:09:40,780 --> 00:09:42,573
[WILLIAM grunts]

179
00:09:48,038 --> 00:09:50,707
[WHITELEY] Maggie,
where's the morning edition?

180
00:09:50,748 --> 00:09:53,210
Oh, well, actually it's--

181
00:09:53,251 --> 00:09:54,919
Oh, I see now.

182
00:09:54,961 --> 00:09:58,798
More frontpage denigration
from my critics, no surprise.

183
00:09:58,840 --> 00:10:01,009
But really, it
doesn't bother me.

184
00:10:01,051 --> 00:10:02,302
Kindly bring it here.

185
00:10:02,344 --> 00:10:06,097
Oh, very well. But don't
say I didn't warn you.

186
00:10:07,140 --> 00:10:09,976
[ROBINSON] Good morning
to you, Mister Whiteley.

187
00:10:10,018 --> 00:10:13,146
[WHITELEY] Inspector Robinson
and Sturridge, same to you.

188
00:10:13,188 --> 00:10:15,481
Come, sit down and
join us for breakfast.

189
00:10:15,523 --> 00:10:17,483
We've only just started eating.

190
00:10:17,525 --> 00:10:18,526
[ROBINSON]
You eat breakfast together

191
00:10:18,568 --> 00:10:20,737
like one big happy
family, I see.

192
00:10:20,778 --> 00:10:22,530
Rather cosy, I must confess.

193
00:10:22,572 --> 00:10:24,824
This is pretty much
standard procedure here.

194
00:10:24,866 --> 00:10:28,411
I'm sure you'll get used to our
ways quite quickly, Inspector.

195
00:10:28,452 --> 00:10:30,538
Maggie makes the
most delicious pies.

196
00:10:30,580 --> 00:10:31,998
Wait till you have one.

197
00:10:32,040 --> 00:10:35,168
[ROBINSON]
I certainly look forward to it.

198
00:10:35,210 --> 00:10:36,502
[SAM]
Good morning, Adam.

199
00:10:36,544 --> 00:10:37,879
We have guests.

200
00:10:37,921 --> 00:10:40,673
You are Mister Robinson
and Sturridge?

201
00:10:40,715 --> 00:10:42,884
And good morning
to you, too, Marcus.

202
00:10:42,926 --> 00:10:45,345
[STURRIDGE]
Ah, good to see you, Sam.

203
00:10:45,387 --> 00:10:46,429
Hope you don't mind,

204
00:10:46,470 --> 00:10:49,266
but we'll be joining you
for breakfast today.

205
00:10:49,307 --> 00:10:51,017
Where's Maggie?

206
00:10:51,059 --> 00:10:53,353
Most likely making a
big fuss and snipping out

207
00:10:53,395 --> 00:10:56,480
any defamation towards me
in this morning's paper.

208
00:10:56,522 --> 00:11:00,193
Don't tease. She's
just worried about you.

209
00:11:00,235 --> 00:11:02,695
[SAM sighs] There's no
end to the threatening

210
00:11:02,737 --> 00:11:03,988
letters you receive.

211
00:11:04,030 --> 00:11:08,326
And only yesterday, there
was an attempt on your life.

212
00:11:08,368 --> 00:11:11,871
And she's not the
only one. I worry, too.

213
00:11:13,831 --> 00:11:15,208
[WHITELEY]
Listen.

214
00:11:15,250 --> 00:11:17,501
I understand your
concerns, Samuel.

215
00:11:17,543 --> 00:11:20,422
However, I'm not the sort
of man who allows his will

216
00:11:20,463 --> 00:11:22,006
to be shaped by pressure.

217
00:11:22,048 --> 00:11:24,301
Or backs down to cowards
who level threats.

218
00:11:24,342 --> 00:11:26,636
I know, but still...

219
00:11:26,677 --> 00:11:28,012
[WHITELEY]
As soon as my bill passes,

220
00:11:28,054 --> 00:11:30,765
things will change
for the better. Trust me.

221
00:11:30,807 --> 00:11:34,727
A fair and equal world is coming
where people can truly be free.

222
00:11:35,937 --> 00:11:37,730
I trust you. But I just--

223
00:11:37,772 --> 00:11:39,316
We know you're proud of him!

224
00:11:39,357 --> 00:11:40,733
That brother of
yours is going to

225
00:11:40,775 --> 00:11:42,819
single-handedly
change this nation!

226
00:11:42,860 --> 00:11:44,612
That's why we've taken
on the responsibility

227
00:11:44,654 --> 00:11:46,364
of making sure he's protected.

228
00:11:46,406 --> 00:11:47,991
Things have proved challenging,

229
00:11:48,032 --> 00:11:51,161
but our investigation into
the threats is proceeding,

230
00:11:51,202 --> 00:11:53,704
so please, try not to worry.

231
00:11:54,705 --> 00:11:56,124
[SAM grunts]

232
00:11:57,625 --> 00:11:58,835
[WHITELEY]
You're sure?

233
00:11:58,876 --> 00:12:02,088
I wish you'd be able to
come to the ceremony today.

234
00:12:02,130 --> 00:12:06,968
I'm ever sorry. Do come
back safely. Promise me?

235
00:12:08,011 --> 00:12:10,721
Of course, Sam. Off I go, then.

236
00:12:16,769 --> 00:12:18,813
He has the inspectors
looking after him.

237
00:12:18,855 --> 00:12:21,607
He should be perfectly fine.

238
00:12:29,115 --> 00:12:31,451
The dream we worked
tirelessly to bring about

239
00:12:31,493 --> 00:12:33,828
has nearly come to fruition.

240
00:12:33,870 --> 00:12:35,413
You will be making
your remarks after

241
00:12:35,455 --> 00:12:37,999
Mayor Dempster briefly
says a few words.

242
00:12:38,041 --> 00:12:39,542
One last thing.

243
00:12:39,583 --> 00:12:42,462
After the ceremony is
completed and park is opened,

244
00:12:42,504 --> 00:12:45,965
the guests you invited
will be welcome to see it.

245
00:12:46,007 --> 00:12:49,427
Excellent news. Thank you.

246
00:12:49,469 --> 00:12:51,429
I can hardly wait.

247
00:12:52,889 --> 00:12:54,682
[MARCUS gasps]
No.

248
00:12:54,724 --> 00:12:56,100
This is all wrong.

249
00:12:56,142 --> 00:13:00,938
What the devil is going on here?
This isn't what we planned.

250
00:13:00,980 --> 00:13:04,108
Those are the MPs
who opposed his bill!

251
00:13:04,150 --> 00:13:06,194
What're they doing here?

252
00:13:06,236 --> 00:13:07,404
If they haven't yet,

253
00:13:07,445 --> 00:13:10,407
soon they'll talk the mayor
into siding with them.

254
00:13:10,448 --> 00:13:11,991
This whole ceremony
will turn into

255
00:13:12,033 --> 00:13:15,036
a public execution
of Mister Whiteley!

256
00:13:16,246 --> 00:13:18,956
[DEMPSTER] Ladies and gentlemen,
I am Mayor Dempster.

257
00:13:18,998 --> 00:13:21,501
I trust you're doing
well this fine day.

258
00:13:21,543 --> 00:13:23,002
Standing here in front of you,

259
00:13:23,044 --> 00:13:25,046
it brings me great
pleasure to announce

260
00:13:25,088 --> 00:13:28,966
this lovely park
is now open to the public.

261
00:13:29,008 --> 00:13:31,719
But looking back,
the journey of completing it,

262
00:13:31,761 --> 00:13:35,515
well, it certainly wasn't an
easy task to say the least.

263
00:13:35,557 --> 00:13:38,935
Public safety concerns,
pollution issues,

264
00:13:38,976 --> 00:13:41,645
but clearly, the most
challenging setback

265
00:13:41,687 --> 00:13:44,023
was none other than the
"champion of the people,"

266
00:13:44,065 --> 00:13:46,276
MP Adam Whiteley!

267
00:13:46,317 --> 00:13:49,779
To begin with, he revoked
the plans for this park

268
00:13:49,820 --> 00:13:53,283
after they had
already been approved!

269
00:13:53,324 --> 00:13:54,742
[TOWNSMAN 19A]
Whiteley did that?

270
00:13:54,784 --> 00:13:57,328
[TOWNSMAN 19B]
Is that true?

271
00:13:57,370 --> 00:13:58,704
[DEMPSTER]
Not only that--

272
00:13:58,746 --> 00:14:02,375
he then had the nerve to force
newly designed plans on us.

273
00:14:02,417 --> 00:14:05,420
Drawn up by him, of course!

274
00:14:05,462 --> 00:14:07,505
And do you know the result?

275
00:14:07,547 --> 00:14:11,092
Not only did the park far exceed
its original allocated budget,

276
00:14:11,134 --> 00:14:13,386
but additionally,
all construction efforts

277
00:14:13,428 --> 00:14:16,347
nearly came to a
complete and utter halt!

278
00:14:16,389 --> 00:14:19,058
Despite the
obstacles he created,

279
00:14:19,100 --> 00:14:23,354
we were undaunted and saw the
project to completion for you.

280
00:14:23,396 --> 00:14:26,941
To give the people this
beautiful public space.

281
00:14:26,983 --> 00:14:29,527
Yes, there was a great
amount of influence

282
00:14:29,569 --> 00:14:32,905
in its creation, but it
wasn't from Mister Whiteley.

283
00:14:32,947 --> 00:14:35,283
If you want to know who
you should really thank,

284
00:14:35,325 --> 00:14:38,578
it's the members of
parliament with us today!

285
00:14:38,620 --> 00:14:40,163
[TOWNSPEOPLE murmur]

286
00:14:40,204 --> 00:14:41,956
And now, I'll ask Mister Spencer

287
00:14:41,998 --> 00:14:44,792
to say a few words on
behalf of these gentlemen.

288
00:14:46,710 --> 00:14:50,298
My fellow Londoners,
before I begin, let me say

289
00:14:50,340 --> 00:14:53,926
it pains me to bring this up
on such a glorious day.

290
00:14:53,968 --> 00:14:57,514
But I must take this opportunity
to address something.

291
00:14:57,555 --> 00:15:00,724
Concerning the park
and his election reform bill,

292
00:15:00,766 --> 00:15:02,726
the man you see
sitting over there

293
00:15:02,768 --> 00:15:05,688
has been every bit a dictator.

294
00:15:05,729 --> 00:15:08,483
He pushed for his vision,
despite not reaching consensus

295
00:15:08,525 --> 00:15:09,651
in the House of Commons,

296
00:15:09,692 --> 00:15:12,320
and chose to submit the
bill of his own accord.

297
00:15:12,362 --> 00:15:14,822
Furthermore, there has
been reformation dialogue

298
00:15:14,864 --> 00:15:16,782
proceeding behind closed doors.

299
00:15:16,824 --> 00:15:19,243
But the efforts of the
House of Lords has been derailed

300
00:15:19,285 --> 00:15:21,621
by none other
than Mister Whiteley!

301
00:15:21,663 --> 00:15:25,291
In short, the person who's
standing in the way of reform

302
00:15:25,333 --> 00:15:29,295
is none other than
Mister Whiteley himself!

303
00:15:29,337 --> 00:15:31,548
[MARCUS] They're
placing the blame on him?

304
00:15:31,589 --> 00:15:33,883
This is simply absurd!

305
00:15:33,924 --> 00:15:35,885
Regular negotiations
with the House of Lords

306
00:15:35,926 --> 00:15:39,722
would have taken at least 30
years before reforms passed!

307
00:15:39,763 --> 00:15:41,724
He is risking his life
and going alone

308
00:15:41,765 --> 00:15:44,768
for the benefit of
all the citizenry!

309
00:15:44,810 --> 00:15:48,731
Good people, I ask only that you
please decide for yourselves.

310
00:15:48,772 --> 00:15:49,982
Who here on this stage

311
00:15:50,024 --> 00:15:52,485
is truly acting in the best
interest of the citizens

312
00:15:52,527 --> 00:15:55,697
and not simply paying
them lip service?

313
00:15:55,738 --> 00:15:58,157
Consider this
for God and country!

314
00:15:58,199 --> 00:16:01,869
[DEMPSTER] Thank you very
much for that, Mister Spencer.

315
00:16:01,911 --> 00:16:03,829
What he said, is it true?

316
00:16:03,871 --> 00:16:05,373
I'm not sure.

317
00:16:05,415 --> 00:16:09,001
Let's wait and hear
what Whiteley has to say.

318
00:16:09,043 --> 00:16:10,794
[DEMPSTER]
Now we will hear some remarks

319
00:16:10,836 --> 00:16:14,173
from the infamous
Mister Whiteley.

320
00:16:26,561 --> 00:16:28,396
Right.

321
00:16:28,438 --> 00:16:30,648
What you heard from the
Mayor and Mister Spencer

322
00:16:30,690 --> 00:16:31,982
concerning this park,

323
00:16:32,024 --> 00:16:34,944
I would like to take a
moment to respond to.

324
00:16:36,820 --> 00:16:38,615
All of it is the truth.

325
00:16:38,656 --> 00:16:40,533
[TOWNSPEOPLE gasp, murmur]

326
00:16:40,575 --> 00:16:41,325
[TOWNSMAN 19E]
What?

327
00:16:41,367 --> 00:16:42,285
[TOWNSMAN 19F]
Are you serious?

328
00:16:42,326 --> 00:16:43,911
[TOWNSWOMAN 19B]
You aren't denying it?

329
00:16:43,953 --> 00:16:46,163
[TOWNSMAN 19G]
We believed in you!

330
00:16:46,205 --> 00:16:50,501
My ego compelled me to step in
with my own plans for the park.

331
00:16:50,543 --> 00:16:53,087
Also, I was aware
that both houses

332
00:16:53,129 --> 00:16:54,422
were at work behind the scenes

333
00:16:54,464 --> 00:16:57,300
making efforts toward
bringing about reforms.

334
00:16:57,341 --> 00:17:01,763
However, I nonetheless proceeded
to propose my own bill.

335
00:17:01,804 --> 00:17:04,932
Lastly, I would like to
apologise for the delay

336
00:17:04,974 --> 00:17:10,020
regarding the park's completion.
The blame is on me.

337
00:17:10,062 --> 00:17:12,856
And that is all I have
to say on the matter.

338
00:17:12,898 --> 00:17:14,776
[TOWNSMAN 19H]
What? That's all?

339
00:17:14,817 --> 00:17:16,736
[TOWNSMAN 19I]
You haven't explained a thing!

340
00:17:16,778 --> 00:17:19,530
[TOWNSMAN 19J] He must've been
involved in something shady,

341
00:17:19,572 --> 00:17:21,324
otherwise he would
have said more.

342
00:17:21,365 --> 00:17:24,619
[TOWNSMAN 19K]
My word, what a disappointment.

343
00:17:30,667 --> 00:17:32,126
[MARCUS]
But why?

344
00:17:32,168 --> 00:17:34,712
Why didn't you
defend yourself, sir?

345
00:17:34,754 --> 00:17:35,921
[WHITELEY]
There's no need.

346
00:17:35,963 --> 00:17:37,799
[MARCUS] But you could've
outlined your intentions

347
00:17:37,840 --> 00:17:41,302
when designing this place!
What you wanted to achieve!

348
00:17:41,344 --> 00:17:43,137
If you hadn't stepped in,
this would have ended up

349
00:17:43,179 --> 00:17:45,222
being just like any other park!

350
00:17:45,264 --> 00:17:48,058
I'm sure the audience would
have understood your reasons!

351
00:17:48,100 --> 00:17:49,435
Most of all,

352
00:17:49,477 --> 00:17:53,897
what infuriates me is how those
MPs tarnished your honour!

353
00:17:53,939 --> 00:17:58,402
[WHITELEY laughs]
Come now, "my honour"?

354
00:17:58,444 --> 00:18:02,448
Saving it was never the
priority. Forget all that.

355
00:18:02,490 --> 00:18:03,658
Look, they're here.

356
00:18:03,700 --> 00:18:06,076
[MARCUS]
Hm?

357
00:18:06,118 --> 00:18:08,120
[WHITELEY]
My guests of honour have arrived

358
00:18:08,162 --> 00:18:10,623
and are enjoying the grounds.

359
00:18:10,665 --> 00:18:11,874
[CHILDREN laughing]

360
00:18:11,915 --> 00:18:14,001
[BOY 19A]
This is brilliant!

361
00:18:14,043 --> 00:18:17,254
They made sure the entire
estate was built on one level!

362
00:18:17,296 --> 00:18:20,174
So we should be
safe playing out here!

363
00:18:20,216 --> 00:18:23,469
[BOY 19A] Hey! I'll race
you over to that tree!

364
00:18:23,511 --> 00:18:24,970
[CARETAKER 19A]
Children, children!

365
00:18:25,012 --> 00:18:26,681
Stop being reckless!

366
00:18:26,723 --> 00:18:28,725
You don't mean...

367
00:18:28,766 --> 00:18:31,394
This is the vision I strove for.

368
00:18:31,435 --> 00:18:33,730
Seeing the smiles
on their faces.

369
00:18:33,771 --> 00:18:34,689
[CHILDREN laugh]

370
00:18:34,731 --> 00:18:35,815
[WHITELEY]
Based on their expressions,

371
00:18:35,857 --> 00:18:38,317
I've achieved my goal.

372
00:18:38,359 --> 00:18:40,236
Now I see.

373
00:18:44,990 --> 00:18:50,037
[CHILDREN laughing]

374
00:18:52,832 --> 00:18:55,376
[WHITELEY grunts]
Aren't you...?

375
00:18:55,418 --> 00:18:57,545
Why, Lord Moriarty.

376
00:18:57,587 --> 00:19:01,424
Quite a lovely day. Wouldn't
you agree, Mister Whiteley?

377
00:19:01,465 --> 00:19:05,052
Forgive my abruptness,
but I'd like to have a word.

378
00:19:08,848 --> 00:19:10,892
[WHITELEY]
Tell me, Lord Moriarty,

379
00:19:10,933 --> 00:19:13,143
seeing how you are a member
of the House of Lords,

380
00:19:13,185 --> 00:19:15,145
why would you wish
to speak with me?

381
00:19:15,187 --> 00:19:20,902
Well, actually, I have a request
that only you can fulfil.

382
00:19:22,737 --> 00:19:24,113
What is this?

383
00:19:24,154 --> 00:19:26,990
[ALBERT] Exactly what
you need at this moment:

384
00:19:27,032 --> 00:19:28,409
Irrefutable proof

385
00:19:28,451 --> 00:19:31,746
linking the House of Lords
with known mobsters.

386
00:19:31,788 --> 00:19:33,080
[gasps]

387
00:19:33,122 --> 00:19:34,874
[ALBERT] As long as
you have these documents,

388
00:19:34,916 --> 00:19:38,043
you'll be able to conduct
your backroom dealings.

389
00:19:38,085 --> 00:19:40,922
Why would you trust me
with something so incendiary

390
00:19:40,963 --> 00:19:42,673
when it compromises you?

391
00:19:42,715 --> 00:19:44,842
[ALBERT] You may find
this difficult to believe,

392
00:19:44,884 --> 00:19:47,470
but regarding how the
House of Lords operates,

393
00:19:47,511 --> 00:19:51,265
I've grown, shall
we say, disenchanted.

394
00:19:51,307 --> 00:19:53,893
And besides, based on
everything I've witnessed,

395
00:19:53,935 --> 00:19:57,980
I'm certain it won't be used
for nefarious purposes.

396
00:19:58,021 --> 00:20:02,485
Once I made that determination,
I decided to entrust it to you.

397
00:20:02,526 --> 00:20:04,153
I see.

398
00:20:04,194 --> 00:20:08,324
[ALBERT] Hm. As long as your
character remains uncorrupted,

399
00:20:08,365 --> 00:20:12,787
you will have my continued
support. That is all.

400
00:20:22,421 --> 00:20:26,258
I really do find him to be
quite a pleasant man, William.

401
00:20:26,300 --> 00:20:28,552
Thanks to the ceremony
that you orchestrated,

402
00:20:28,594 --> 00:20:29,929
we were able to make certain

403
00:20:29,971 --> 00:20:32,807
Mister Whiteley's
humanity was intact.

404
00:20:32,849 --> 00:20:37,687
Indeed. However, this power
we've handed to him...

405
00:20:37,728 --> 00:20:40,857
It was a difficult decision
that had to be made--

406
00:20:40,898 --> 00:20:45,194
for him to accept the documents,
but also for us to provide them.

407
00:20:45,235 --> 00:20:49,323
Though, if we had
chosen to do nothing,

408
00:20:49,365 --> 00:20:54,203
without this newfound influence,
he would surely be eliminated.

409
00:20:54,244 --> 00:20:56,873
When it comes to the cost
of meeting an objective,

410
00:20:56,914 --> 00:20:59,333
there's always
a price to be paid.

411
00:20:59,375 --> 00:21:01,043
All the more true
when it comes to

412
00:21:01,084 --> 00:21:04,755
the colossal goal of
transforming this nation.

413
00:21:04,797 --> 00:21:06,632
Am I right?

414
00:21:09,010 --> 00:21:12,680
[WILLIAM]
Yes, you are, Brother.

415
00:21:16,851 --> 00:21:18,644
Shall we?

416
00:21:27,319 --> 00:21:29,572
[POLICEMAN 19D]
No! Wait! I did as you said!

417
00:21:29,613 --> 00:21:32,074
I killed the criminal
who went after Whiteley!

418
00:21:32,115 --> 00:21:33,659
What more do you want?

419
00:21:33,701 --> 00:21:37,454
Please, spare me!

420
00:21:43,085 --> 00:21:44,294
[HARRY]
Very good.

421
00:21:44,336 --> 00:21:46,171
As per Mister Milverton's
instructions,

422
00:21:46,213 --> 00:21:49,508
we're to make sure that this
officer's body is never found.

423
00:21:49,550 --> 00:21:52,053
Dump him somewhere suitable.

424
00:21:52,094 --> 00:21:54,764
As for our next target...

425
00:21:55,973 --> 00:21:59,894
Heh. The MP's brother,

426
00:21:59,936 --> 00:22:03,355
little Sam Whiteley.

427
00:23:34,655 --> 00:23:37,033
[LESTRADE] Next time on
Moriarty the Patriot  :

428
00:23:37,074 --> 00:23:39,827
"The White Knight
of London, Act Two."
